## How authentication works right now
### Flow 1: Register or Login
The browser sends a POST request to /auth/register or /auth/login with a JSON body (username, email, password).

AuthController receives it. Spring automatically validates the fields (the @NotBlank, @Email annotations on the DTOs) — if anything is missing or malformed, Spring rejects it before the controller code even runs.

AuthController calls AuthService.register() or AuthService.login().

Inside AuthService:

For register: checks that the email and username aren't already taken (via UserRepository), hashes the password using BCrypt, saves a new row to the users table.
For login: looks up the user by email, then calls passwordEncoder.matches() to compare the submitted password against the stored BCrypt hash.
Either way, if successful, it calls the private generateTokens(user) method which:

Calls JwtService.generateAccessToken(user) — creates a signed JWT string containing the user's ID and email, expires in 15 minutes
Creates a new RefreshToken object with a random UUID, sets expiry to 7 days from now, saves it to the refresh_tokens table
Returns both tokens back up to AuthController
AuthController then:

Puts the refresh token UUID into an HttpOnly cookie (the browser stores it but JavaScript cannot read it)
Puts the access token JWT in the response body as JSON
The frontend receives the access token in JSON and holds it in memory. The browser automatically stores the cookie.

### Flow 2: Making a protected API request
The frontend attaches the access token to every request as a header: Authorization: Bearer <the JWT string>.

Before the request reaches any controller, it passes through JwtAuthenticationFilter. This filter runs on every single request. It:

Reads the Authorization header
If there's no header, or it doesn't start with Bearer , it skips and passes the request along unauthenticated
If there is a token, it passes it to JwtService.getUserIdFromToken() which validates the HMAC256 signature and expiry, then extracts the user ID from the token's "subject" field
Loads the User from the database using that ID
Places the User object into Spring's SecurityContextHolder — this is how Spring knows "this request belongs to this user"
After the filter, the request reaches SecurityConfig's rules. /auth/** is public so it skips the check. Everything else requires authentication. If the filter didn't set a user (bad/missing token), Spring blocks the request with a 401 response before it ever reaches a controller.

If everything is fine, the request reaches the controller. Any controller can get the current user by reading from SecurityContextHolder.

### Flow 3: Refreshing the access token
When the access token expires (after 15 min), the frontend sends a POST to /auth/refresh. It doesn't send any body — the browser automatically includes the HttpOnly cookie.

AuthController reads the cookie value and passes it to AuthService.refresh().

Inside AuthService:

Looks up the refresh token UUID in the refresh_tokens table
If not found — someone sent a fake token, throws an error
Checks the expiresAt column — if past 7 days, throws an error
Deletes the old refresh token row (this is "rotation" — each refresh token can only be used once)
Calls generateTokens(user) again — creates a brand new access token and a brand new refresh token row
The response comes back with a new cookie and a new access token, and the frontend swaps the old one in memory for the new one.

